DEC OSF/1 has a configurable swap method. By default, it is in "eager" swap
mode. That is, there is a softlink in /sbin called swapdefault that points
to your swap partition. This method is "eager" to swap, and preallocates
swap space, so, even if it doesn't really swap, it has allocated the space.
	The second method, called "lazy" swap, involves removing the link, and
rebooting. This method doesn't do such eager preallocation, and you will use
much less swap. However, on occasion, you might run out of swap un-expectedly
and the process that is being to swapped out may crash...

