>do the same thing. For example, 'xcd' is current 5.02Mb.
>
>Is there a memory leak somewhere? If so, are they patches?

It would greatly help to know what version of operating system you are running.
Between V1.2 and V1.3 there has been major changes in swap/paging algorithms, 
and if version is V1.2 I'd recommed doing an upgrade installation instead of
applying any (possible) patches. Also, size of X Server can vary very much 
depending on the applications (and specially X resource usage of applications).
I'd recommend all V1.2 users to upgrade V1.3 if possible! It does not only give
you performance advantage, but V1.3 contains new functionality and applies 
known patches.

Usage, performance and load balancing of swap has improved greatly in V1.3,
and V1.2 also reported usage of Virtual Memory incorrectly at some stage. 
I suspect that numbers you gave are Virtual Memory usage (VSZ), which in 
most cases isn't important; rather consumption of real memory shouldn't be 
too high (RSZ)! I'm running V1.3 and system has been up  64 days (what a 
coinsidence ;-) and VSZ of X is about 16MB while RSS is about 800K-1M. I'm
running very different X applications from 2D to highly bitmap intensive
without any noticebly memory leaks in Xdec. Most of the applications has
been reported (by ps) to have VSZ os 8-15MB's, which propably comes from
that ps commands reports also the VM mapping of shared libraries with usage
of the real VS of process and program itself. That explains why applications
linked against large libraries always have static offset which doesn't
belong to application itself. This means in most cases that VSZ doesn't
mean anything to user if RSS doesn't grow too high. If RSS is what you 
reported, then you certainly have some problem, but I can't replicate any
of that memory usage with any application I've tested in V1.2 or V1.3.

I try to answer with more details if there are further questions.

Best regards,
