In article <anthony.746771254@eden-valley>, anthony@aaii.oz.au (Anthony Baxter) 
writes:
>I had the same problem. I fixed (?) it by doing as people here suggested,
>and removing '/sbin/swapdefault' - this puts it into 'swap overcommitment
>mode', which from my reading of it, means it will behave like any other
>unix when it gets low on swap... this is fine by me. Perhaps someone in
>the know can explain what 'swap overcommitment mode' means, exactly?

In the default `eager' mode, swap space is allocated whenever virtual address
space is created. This pre-allocation of swap space - especially when there's
adequate physical memory - is quite consumptive of swap space. 

By removing the /sbin/swapdefault link and rebooting, you switch to `lazy'
swap mode; swap space is allocated only when the pages are about to be
swapped out.

The down side is that if the kernel can't allocate space to swap a chosen
process, that process gets killed. You avoid this problem by making sure
there's enough swap space. The default behavior was chosen to be the more
conservative approach.
	-Rick
