	The nice thing about OSF/1 is where in SVR4 there's some
incompatibility between SVR3 and BSD, the SVR3 way is chosen. In
OSF/1, you just include the right library for whichever behaviour
you want. Supposedly this works to some extent in Solaris as well,
but to date it doesn't look that impressive.

: > As for GNU, Solaris apparently has enough mass to be better
: > supported than OSF/1.

	OSF/1 doesn't need as much support as Solaris does. :-)

: At the moment. But OSF/1 basically only running on Alphas,
: and much GNU software assumes that pointers fit in ints.
: I've ported Emacs 19 to Alpha (with help from someone at
: DEC), and the 64 bit pointers was the tricky bit, not the OS.
: It's not a big problem, it's just a tedious one, and so
: it's not surprising no-one's in a rush.

	The thing here is not to confuse OS issues with hardware
issues. What I can see happening here is people grumbling about
"OSF" when they actually mean "64 bit machines", fixing all their
code, and then when (if? :-) Sun come out with a 64 bit Sparc,
all the problems will have already been fixed.

	64 bits isn't an OS problem, it's a problem in moving
forward to the future. Not going to 64 bits may solve your cruddy
programming problems, but it's sure going to make life tough for
you further down the track.

	Andrew.

All opinions are my own.

