I have several problems/question with rdump/rrestore on OSF/1 V1.2, 
hopefully some may be fixed in V1.3, but I don't have it yet.

We have tape drives on a SUNOS 4.1.3 system which we backup remote
systems to using rdump. I have come across a few differences between
the way rdump/rrestore work between OSF/1 and Ultrix in conjunction 
with using the SUN drives. We use a regular account called "dumper" 
on OSF/1 & Ultrix to run the backup scripts, rather than the "root" 
account.

Major problem:
- OSF/1 rrestore generates the following errors, when run from the "dumper"
  account, but works ok if run from "root". rrestore is setuid root,
  so I don't believe that it's a permission problem. The command used
  was:  /usr/sbin/rrestore -tf sun:/dev/exa >> filename

         Cannot read single character from socket
         rmt_getb(): read(): Address already in use
         Communication error in connection to remote peer
         Remote restore is aborted.
         
Minor problems:
- OSF/1 rrestore seems to need to communicate with a similarly named
  account on the SUN, and thus an account with the same name with
  appropriate .rhosts file needs to be setup on the SUN for this purpose.
  OSF/1 rdump and Ultrix rdump/rrestore default to using the "root" account 
  and thus only /.rhosts needs to be used.
 
- Any chance of seeing support for username@hostname:/dev/tape type of
  file specification for rdump/rrestore on OSF/1? Ultrix doesn't have it,
  but I can always hope for OSF/1! 

- OSF/1 rdump has difficulty opening the /dev/rrz* devices even if it's
  been setuid'd to root. I still have to change the file protections of 
  the /dev/rrz* files so that it can be opened by the account that 
  runs the "rdump". Anyone know why? On Ultrix all I have to do is setuid
  /etc/rdump and it's able to open the devices.  (In fact I have the 
  same situation with SGI systems...)

Slight problems:
- OSF/1 rrestore seems to printout the output of the "-t" switch to
  the stderr instead of stdout. Thus I need to use a command such as
  "rrestore -tf sun:/dev/exa >>& filename" instead of ">> filename".

- OSF/1 rrestore expects the SUN "rmt" to be in /usr/sbin/rmt, so I've made
  a link on the SUN to /etc/rmt. (Interestingly enough OSF/1 rdump
  checks both /usr/sbin and /etc for SUN rmt!). 

Overall it seems that OSF/1 rdump works OK, but rrestore has the most
problems.

Thanks for any help.

