> Beware of the local:0 DISPLAY setting. This indeed speeds up graphics
> performance, but if the program terminates without closing the
> connection properly (e.g. if killed), it leaves a 1-MB shared memory
> segment behind THAT WON'T DISAPPEAR until it is explicitely deleted!
I think that's because they use a SysV shm connection. And SysV shm
segment's are persistent. They must be, because the programming model
of SysV shm allows, that later created processes can attach a region
to the adress space. These regions must be explicitly deleted.
After the next boot they are away.

Martin

