In article <14966364@MVB.SAIC.COM>, matthews@apg5.saic.com (Cameron H. Matthews) writes:

> [ Having discovered how to put multiple dumps on a single tape the
>   the customer now wishes to get listing of what is on each dump... ]
>
>
>To list the files:
>
>$ restore -t -f /dev/nrmt0h
>$ mt fsf 1
>$ restore -t 
>
>Is there a better way?

Not really.  The problem is that the archive is roughly in two parts; the
directory and the data.  The -t options reads up to the end of the
directory part and stops.  If there is any data, you have to skip
over it to get to the next dump.  If the dump was empty, some versions
will leave you at start of the next dump and the mt trick won't work
consistently.

A solution might be to add an option that will have restore read the
directory and skip to the beginning of the next dump.  Decent tape
drives with a fast file mark search and good software support can
make this a reasonably fast operation.  An alternate solution might
be to add an option to restore to say "read all the directories until
EOT".  Finally, you could just change the behaviour of -t to read the
rest of the archive.

I don't know what assumptions that may break, if any.

An alternate to the mt(1) is dd(1), but I suspect mt is easier.

