We just took delivery of 2 Alpha 400's running OSF/1 1.3.  After a certain
amount of learning I have just about got them configured ok.  However, there
is one rather large problem still that needs to be resolved and I've been
unable to talk to anybody at DEC who knows anything about this stuff.

The problem in a nutshell is that the machines can talk to the local subnet
just fine but cannot talk across the router to the outside world.  This is
clearly a route setting problem, but the resolution of the problem is less
clear.

On our DEC 5000's running Ultrix the routing information is supplied to the
machines by the call:

	/etc/route add default 129.219.144.129 1

in rc.local.  OSF/1 has no such file of course and the equivalent, called
the gateways file in /etc, has an alternate file format.  So far the form
we've been attempting to use is as follows:

	net la.asu.edu gateway 129.219.144.129 metric 1 active

where we're really guessing about the classification of the setup we need
using the final parameter.  The behaviour of the system is as follows.  Upon
boot, or internet services restart, the machine can locate the DNS servers
on our campus from the resolv.conf file just fine.  After about 2-3 minutes
the machines suddenly loses touch with everything outside the subnet.

This form of response appears to match the description of what happens when
the routed gets no feedback from the routing table for a while - it wipes the
routing table.  OK, I can handle this if true.  So, we try one of the other
settings for the gateways file - either passive or external and none of these
successfully establish any links with the world outside our subnet.

I have tried running routed with either the -q or the -s flags to see if the
prescence of the RIP packets makes a difference,  but it does not.

If someone out there has already tackled this business I would really like
to talk to them about it.  Of course once resolved a summary will be posted.
Thanks for your time.

-- 
