DISCLAIMER:	In what follows, I am *not* speaking for Digital.
		I am technically only speaking for myself.  I am
		the DEC OSF/1 Engineering security project leader,
		however, so my words are not completely empty.
		Just remember that I can't actually guarantee any
		action by Digital as a whole.

The 'next' version of DEC OSF/1, V2.0, which is now entering external
field test, has an ENHANCED security mode.  The baselevel which is going
to EFT does not have the ULTRIX compatibility code for crypt16() passwords
in it, but the EFT-update baselevel will (it's already in the source
pool).  That's the good news.

However, DEC OSF/1 V2.0 will not support network-wide sharing of the
authentication data.  That is being planned for a possible future release,
but the plans are not firm for that.  I don't even know for sure which
release yet.  When we do get to add that support, the current planning is
for DCE (and maybe NIS).  There are no current plans to do BIND/Hesiod
support.  Complain via sales rep, account rep, or other avenue into
Product Management if you really need Hesiod support.

SIA has been pitched to OSF, but is currently just a "DEC thing".  The
internal interfaces are not documented because they are still experimental
and subject to change.  We do plan to document them when they mature a bit
more.  We expect that to be in the same release as the DCE+enhanced
security support.

If someone really wants/needs to do the work to add a new security
mechanism before then, we can discuss the issue of providing the internal
documentation which the DCE and DECnet/OSI groups have used.  That would
require a clear understanding of the risks being run in using an interface
spec which could change without notice.  In practice, if the agreement to
release the specs has been reached, we would attempt to give notice to
those involved.

