In article <23mc0sINN5h8@twain.ucs.umass.edu> cht@twain.ucs.umass.edu (Chester Liu) writes:
>
> DEC OSF/1 V1.2 realtime kernel
> What is it, what does it do, and what are the reasons for using it?

It is a version of the OSF/1 kernel with more locks and preemption points
built in than the normal kernel binaries. Running these kernels gives you
access to the realtime functions according to POSIX 1003.4 Draft 11:

 o  Kernel-mode thread preemption

 o  Fixed-priority scheduling policies

 o  Process memory locking

 o  Semaphores

 o  Shared memory

 o  Process communication facilities

> Does it slow programs down?

The realtime kernel has a slightly higher overhead than the normal kernel
due to much more locking and synchronization going on. So, yes, though
I don't know how to quantify it. 10% is probably a good number (I'll
sure get shot if this is way off).

