So far, I have got seven answers, most of them is for DEC OSF. Many
thanks to those who replied.  The information you sent me was very
helpful, and is greatly appreciated.

Here is the conclusion I got to from the pros and cons:

Most people seem to agree that SunOS 4.1.x is (was?) the best and most
stable traditional unix environment around. But if you're new to unix
like me and want a newer and friendlier interface with graphics and
menus, it looks like you have to choose between Solaris and OSF.
Furthermore, rumours say that SunOS (4.1.x?) is going to be pulled out
soon.

Solaris 2.1 (SunOS 5.x ?) seems to be very buggy, Solaris 2.2 might be
better. 
OSF/1 has been very buggy, but v1.3 is considered to be more stable and
closer to "standard" BSD unix than Solaris. OSF is also reported to have
useful, working setup programs and a good X-windows support.

As for the software I need, the amount of internet freeware is growing
rapidly and porting code to OSF/1 is not considered to be too difficult.
CAP for OSF/1 is current being developed, WAIS and 3270 products already
exists. The DEC C compiler is ANSI and considered to be better than
Suns'. 

To conclude: 
For a new unix environment, OSF seems to be a better choice than Solaris.

