Casper H.S. Dik writes
> DEC's incarnation of 4.2 BSD is better known as ``UNIX from Hell''.
> It was the worst 4.2 port ever attempted.

No. Remember there wasn't much to port. BSD grew up on Vaxes
(or Vaxen as you will) - it was pretty much a question of
changing the name. Even today, you can get nearly anything
running under Ultrix with minimal effort.

Get a bit of good PD code, and count the number of #ifdef ULTRIX
versus #ifdef SUN. The former is a lot more straight-down-the-line.

> Don't make me laugh. 
> 
> 4.2 BSD isn't a ``well designed, well structured'' systems.
> It's hacked together.

It depends what your standards are. "Hacked together" is somewhat the  
nature of Unix: the whole philosophy is of "separate bits".

Yes, there are many things which are inelegant. Take ioctls:
an outstandingly primitive way of communicating with device
drivers.  At least, though, with vanilla BSD the behaviour of
most things is well defined, and there is one way and one
way only to set the parity on a tty line, for example.
With all the "new" OSs, you can do it in lots of ways:
there's a BSD style, a SYSV style, a POSIX style, a native
style...

This is true of Solaris SVR4 just as much as OSF/1. Carrying
historical baggage is a hard fact of commerical life. Thus
the new systems are just as messy. Consider mach: probably the
idea of an OO micro-kernel is a good one, but to have any
commercial viability it needed something like 4.3BSD
compatibility, and is a bit of a lumbering giant as a result.

> Solaris 2.x ... is much better structured...

I honestly don't think so, but I'd be interested in any
technical reasons which negate what I've said above.
What do you claim is wrong with BSD that is so terrible?

I said "Just personal opinion though". One reason I like
OSF is that it's actually rather like BSD.  SVR4 is very much
more of a mix. 

> As for GNU, Solaris apparently has enough mass to be better
> supported than OSF/1.

At the moment. But OSF/1 basically only running on Alphas,
and much GNU software assumes that pointers fit in ints.
I've ported Emacs 19 to Alpha (with help from someone at
DEC), and the 64 bit pointers was the tricky bit, not the OS.
It's not a big problem, it's just a tedious one, and so
it's not surprising no-one's in a rush.


Anthony


