Well, to start, an HP 735 *IS* faster than an Alpha.  At least, every
Alpha but the 500S.  I'm currently working with a 3000/400.  I have
several windows opened to a 5000/200, for software we have not yet
ported.  However, on like tasks, the Alpha seems noticably faster.  For
example, I had cascor running on this Alpha at nearly 900,000
connections/sec, whereas on the 5000/200, I never got far above 40,000
(or so it seemed...).  The only explanation that I can pose to you is
that, due to the expanded bus width, your data is now taking TWICE the
space as it was previously.  It could be that you are overflowing memory
into swap space.  Also, keep in mind that the Alpha uses slower memory
than it should, to keep costs to a reasonable level.  Thus, if you work
the cache *too* hard, things might slow a bit (still not to the level of
a DECstation).  One last thing, the tasks that you mentioned are
largerly DISK-intensive.  These will not be getting much speedup on the
Alpha.  The fast thing on the Alpha is, of course, the processor. 


