DEC is not the only vendor shipping OSF/1.  Since OSF does not require
companies that license the source code to use the OSF/1 name in their
productization of the operating system, OSF/1 code may appear in
vendors products without mentioning they are OSF/1 based.  
IBM is shipping OSF/1, but calls it AIX.  AIX/ESA on
their mainframe is OSF/1 and includes the Mach kernel.  AIX 3.2 on the
RS-6000 is OSF/1, but does not use the Mach kernel.  AIX on the PS2 in
the recently announced Workplace OS (OS2, DOS, AIX, all supported by
the OSF/1 implementation of the Mach Kernel as a microkernel) is
OSF/1.  HP-UX is compliant with the OSF/1 AES (specification for API)
and uses some of OSF/1 code (has made public announcements that they
will be fully compliant with OSF/1 Micro kernel technology when we
ship OSF/1 version 1.3 next year).  Hitachi is shipping OSF/1 on their
mainframe and workstation (HJ-OSF/1).  Intel is shipping OSF/1 (a
microkernel implementation) on their Paragon Machine (MPP).  Kendall
Square Research is shipping OSF/1 as KSR1 in their MPP machine.
Tandem has announce that they will use both SV4 and OSF/1 in their
next fualt tolerant OS.

To date over 80 source code licenses have been executed by endusers,
ISV's and system vendors.  More vendors will announce OSF/1 based
operating system in the coming year.

Keep in mind that system vendors, endusers, and ISV's do not switch
operating environments quickly or lightly (there is the installed base
to consider :-)  ).

OSF/1 passes SVID-3, is conformant to the POSIX committees and is one
of the first operating systems to achieve XPG4 from X/Open.

So when people tell you that no one is shipping OSF/1 they are wrong,
DEC, HP, IBM, Hitachi, and soon many others are, or will shortly be
shipping OSF/1.

FYI,
Bob Goldschneider


