|>If you want to be portable, there's really only one choice: SVR4.
|>
|>Casper
|>

I have heard this said before and I would like to take issue with it. 

We provide a software system called ADAM for astronomical data processing. This
was developed on VAX/VMS and over the last couple of years it has been ported
to DECstations running Ultrix and Suns running SunOS 4.x. We found these two
operating systems to be sufficiently similar that we did not constantly complain
about stupid differences, yet sufficiently different that we were always aware 
that we must not fall into the trap of writing machine specific code - not that
we needed much reminding after years of working in a pure VMS environment.

We are now nearing the end of porting our code to run on OSF/1 on Alphas and
Solaris on Suns. The port to Solaris was by far the most work, the port to OSF/1
being easy in comparison. This is not because the Ultrix code was in better shape
than the SunOS code as more than 99% of it is identical. I do not thing that
Solaris is a bad operating system. It has many features that are far superior to
SunOS. What really annoys me is the many little ways that Sun could have made the
transition easier, but did not. This is not lessened by the fact that DEC have 
made it relatively simple to port from DECstations to Alphas (completely 
different hardware), yet Suns have managed to make it harder to port on the
SAME hardware.

If you currently are running SunOS (version 4) and are dreading the port to
Solaris, I suggest that you consider buying Alphas instead. I believe that it
will be easier to port from SunOS to OSF/1 than from SunOS to Solaris. I am 
perfectly serious about this, but only if you were going to be buying 
new machines anyway.

If you want to be portable there really is only one option - write portable code.
Our DECstations don't run SVR4, our Alphas don't run SVR4 our HPs don't run SVR4
our Sparcstation 1+ doesn't run SVR4 (though it will soon) and my PC doesn't run
SVR4.

All of the above is based on my own experience. Others may well have different
or contradictory experiences that are equally valid.

In the above: SunOS = SunOS 4.x, Solaris = SunOS 5.x
Calling a spade, a spade, is all very well, but not when one of then is a
pick axe.

Peter Allan
Starlink project
Rutherford Appleton Laboratory


